    "category": "correctness",
    "affected_api": "cvxpy.atoms.sigma_max.sigma_max._grad",
    "observed_behavior": "Calling `.grad` for `sigma_max` with non-square (rectangular) matrices crashes with a `ValueError` originating from `LA.svd(values[0])` because it uses `full_matrices=True` by default, leading to misaligned inner dimensions during gradient computation.",
    "expected_behavior": "For non-square matrices, gradient computation should not crash and the analytical gradient should match the numerical gradient to machine precision; implemented by using economy-size SVD: `LA.svd(values[0], full_matrices=False)` in `sigma_max._grad()`.",
    "manifestation": "exception",
    "trigger_conditions": [
      "Calling `.grad` on `sigma_max` with non-square (rectangular) matrices",
      "Inside `sigma_max._grad`, computing SVD via `LA.svd(values[0])` (default `full_matrices=True`)",
      "Rectangular matrices where dimensions differ (non-square shapes)"
    ],
    "input_output_examples": [],
    "oracle_clues": [
      "PR states: \u201cFix a `ValueError` crash when calling `.grad` on `sigma_max` with non-square matrices.\u201d",
      "Root cause: \u201cin `LA.svd(values[0])` within `sigma_max._grad()`, which uses `full_matrices=True` by default.\u201d",
      "Mechanism: \u201cFor rectangular matrices where dimensions differ, this produces full unitary matrices with misaligned inner dimensions during gradient computation.\u201d",
      "Fix: use `LA.svd(values[0], full_matrices=False)` (economy-size SVD).",
      "Verified after fix: \u201canalytical gradient now perfectly matches the numerical gradient to machine precision for non-square matrices.\u201d"
    ]
  },
  "numerical_characteristics": {
    "dtype": null,
    "precision": "machine precision",
    "magnitude": null,
    "tolerance": null,
    "category_specific_evidence": [
      "Fix is to use `LA.svd(values[0], full_matrices=False)` in `sigma_max._grad()`; `LA.svd(values[0])` default `full_matrices=True` caused a crash for non-square matrices.",
      "Root cause: for rectangular matrices where dimensions differ, full unitary matrices with misaligned inner dimensions were produced during gradient computation, preventing access to gradients for rectangular weight matrices commonly used in machine learning and control theory.",
      "Verified after fix: analytical gradient now perfectly matches the numerical gradient to machine precision for non-square matrices.",
      "Added gradient test coverage for `sigma_max` on non-square shapes: `(2, 3)` (`sigma_max_nonsquare`) and `(4, 2)` (`sigma_max_tall`) in `cvxpy/tests/test_grad.py`."
    ],
    "reproduction_risks": [
      "Reproduction requires non-square (rectangular) input matrices; square matrices may not trigger the misalignment/crash described.",
      "The bug is specifically in the gradient path (`sigma_max._grad()` calling `LA.svd(values[0])` with default `full_matrices=True`); reproducing only the forward value `cp.sigma_max(x)` may not demonstrate the issue.",
      "Reproduction should include gradient checking comparing analytical and numerical gradients, since the only stated correctness criterion is matching \u201cto machine precision\u201d for non-square matrices. "
